<h2>Two statutes, one gate</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail is a creature of the general criminal procedure code — Section 482 BNSS empowers the Court of Session and the High Court to direct that a person apprehending arrest be released on bail in the event of arrest. The NDPS Act is a special statute with its own bail philosophy: Section 37 declares every offence under the Act cognizable and non-bailable, and for offences involving commercial quantity, and certain others, permits release on bail only if the twin conditions are met. Where a special statute imposes such conditions, they travel into every form of release the court considers — including release directed in advance of arrest. The practical result is a two-track system defined by quantity.</p>

